LDAP injection occurs when untrusted input reaches LDAP query APIs as filter/query text.
This rule focuses on LDAP lookup/search calls in JNDI, UnboundID, and Spring LDAP operations.

Vulnerable example:

```java
import javax.naming.directory.DirContext;
import javax.naming.directory.SearchControls;

public class LdapService {
    public void find(DirContext ctx, String filterFromRequest) throws Exception {
        // filterFromRequest is untrusted
        ctx.search("ou=users,dc=example,dc=com", filterFromRequest, new SearchControls()); // VULNERABLE
    }
}
```

Safe example:

```java
import javax.naming.directory.DirContext;
import javax.naming.directory.SearchControls;

public class LdapService {
    public void findByUid(DirContext ctx, String uidFromRequest) throws Exception {
        if (uidFromRequest == null || !uidFromRequest.matches("[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}")) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid uid");
        }

        String filter = "(uid={0})";
        Object[] args = new Object[]{uidFromRequest};
        ctx.search("ou=users,dc=example,dc=com", filter, args, new SearchControls());
    }
}
```

Key vulnerable patterns covered by this rule include `DirContext/LdapContext.search`, `lookup`,
UnboundID `LDAPConnection.search`, and Spring LDAP search/lookup APIs with tainted query data.

